Which of the following is the correct implementation of the `find_min` function in a binary search tree?
A. tree_ptr find_min(element_type x, SEARCH_TREE T) { if(T == NULL) return NULL; else if(T->left != NULL) return find_min(x, T->left); else return T; }
B. tree_ptr find_min(element_type x, SEARCH_TREE T) { if(T == NULL) return NULL; else if(T->right != NULL) return find_min(x, T->right); else return T; }
C. tree_ptr find_min(element_type x, SEARCH_TREE T) { if(T == NULL) return NULL; else if(T->left != NULL) return find_min(x, T->right); else return T; }
D. tree_ptr find_min(element_type x, SEARCH_TREE T) { if(T == NULL) return NULL; else return T; }

Which option is correct? Explain briefly.

To compute L1 - L2, you can iterate through L1 and check if each element is present in L2. If an element is not found in L2, it belongs to L1 - L2. Here's a pseudocode example:

```python
def compute_difference(L1, L2):
    result = []
    for item in L1:
        if item not in L2:
            result.append(item)
    return result
```
This approach has a time complexity of O(m + n), where m is the length of L1 and n is the length of L2.
